---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "oath protein powder",
      "oath protein",
      "oath protein review"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Whey Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-whey-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker="Brand Review" badge="Label-Checked" author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Oath Protein Powder: Clear Whey That Drinks Like Juice</Fragment>
      Oath Nutrition comes from the company behind BlenderBottle, and its clear whey has become one of the most visible on Target and Amazon shelves. Here is what is actually in it, which Oath product to buy, who should skip it, and what it costs per 25 g of protein.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#who-makes-oath">Who Makes Oath</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in Oath Clear Whey</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Lineup</a>
      <a href="#which-one">Which Oath Protein to Buy</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g of Protein</a>
      <a href="#testing">Testing and Quality Claims</a>
      <a href="#skip">Who Should Skip Oath</a>
      <a href="#compare">How Oath Compares</a>
      <a href="#using">Getting the Most From It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Oath Clear Whey Isolate is a light, juice-style whey isolate with 20 g of protein in about 100 calories, sweetened with sucralose and priced well when it is on sale. It is a good clear whey, not a great value whey, and it carries no third-party certification we could confirm. Buy it for a refreshing shake, not for the best protein per dollar.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            Oath is a real, established protein brand, and its flagship Clear Whey Isolate is a competent example of the clear whey category. Retailer labels show about 20 g of protein from whey protein isolate in roughly 100 to 110 calories, with about 2 g of carbohydrate and around 1 g of sugar or less, depending on flavor. It mixes into a thin, fruit-flavored drink rather than a milkshake, which is the whole point of clear whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where Oath falls short is the same place most clear wheys fall short: 20 g per scoop and a price that only looks good on sale. At a typical shelf price of around 40 dollars for 18 servings, a 25 g dose of protein costs roughly 2.80 dollars. Oath runs frequent promotions, and on deal it can drop to around 1.40 to 2 dollars per 25 g, which is competitive for a clear isolate. At full price, a standard whey gives you more protein for less money.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other thing to know before buying is verification. Oath says, in retailer Q&A, that its clear whey is made in the United States in cGMP-certified facilities and undergoes third-party testing. We could not find an independent certification such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice attached to Oath products. That does not mean the product is bad. It does mean tested athletes should look elsewhere.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A good clear whey to buy on sale, not a value whey or a tested-sport whey.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Buy Oath Clear Whey Isolate if you want a light, refreshing alternative to milkshake-style protein and can catch it on promotion. Buy the regular Oath Whey Protein if you want 25 g per scoop. Skip both if you need a certified product or the lowest cost per gram.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            This review is built on Oath's own product pages, retailer supplement panels and published trade coverage. We have not tasted or lab-tested Oath products ourselves; where we mention taste, foam or mixing, it is buyer feedback reported as such. For the full field of whey powders ranked on value, dose and verification, see our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-makes-oath" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Makes Oath, and What It Sells
          </h2>

          <p>
            Oath Nutrition is sold by Trove Nutrition, a subsidiary of Trove Brands, the privately held Utah company behind BlenderBottle and Owala. That background explains a lot about the brand: it sits naturally next to shaker bottles in sporting goods and big-box stores, and it has the retail relationships to land on Target, Walmart, Academy and Amazon quickly. Trove describes Oath as a sports performance brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not confuse it with OATH, a separate oat-based dairy alternative brand that also uses the name. When people search for Oath protein powder, they almost always mean Oath Nutrition and its clear whey. The two companies are unrelated, and the oat milk brand does not sell a whey protein powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            Oath's range at the time of writing centers on Clear Protein, its clear whey isolate, sold in 18 and 20 serving pouches direct, 18 serving tubs at retailers such as Target and Academy, newer 15 serving tubs at Walmart according to trade coverage from Stack3d, and single-serve packets. Flavors listed on the brand's site include Frosty Lemonade, Coconut Colada, Coco Razz, Icy Blue Razz, Island Mango, Sunrise Passion Punch, Raspberry Lemonade and Brazilian Limeade, with retailers also listing Watermelon Fresca and seasonal limited editions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beyond clear whey, Oath sells a regular Whey Protein in dessert flavors, a canned Sparkling Energy plus Protein drink launched in April 2026 with 10 g of protein and 100 mg of caffeine, plus electrolytes, a pre-workout and creatine. Trove has described Oath as the best-selling clear protein at Target and a category leader on Amazon in its press materials. That is a brand claim, but its retail footprint is real and easy to see.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is in Oath Clear Whey
          </h2>

          <p>
            Target's listing for the Island Mango tub gives the clearest panel we found at the time of writing: a 26.3 g scoop with 100 calories, 20 g of protein, 2 g of total carbohydrate, 1 g of sugar, 0.5 g of fat, 5 mg of cholesterol and 10 mg of sodium. The product description across flavors claims 100 to 110 calories and less than 1 g of sugar, so treat the exact figures as flavor dependent. Oath's own site describes every flavor as having 20 g of complete protein and no added sugar.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein is whey protein isolate, which Oath describes as grass-fed and put through an ultra-filtration process. Clear whey is typically made by acidifying and further filtering isolate so it dissolves into a thin, translucent drink rather than a creamy shake. That is why Oath lists acids such as malic acid and citric acid on its panels, and why clear whey tastes more like a sports drink than a milkshake.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sweetener on the Island Mango panel is sucralose. Oath's Frosty Lemonade listing describes the product as flavored with natural and artificial flavors. If you avoid sucralose or artificial flavors, check the specific flavor before you buy, because formulas vary across the range. Some flavors also contain coconut milk powder, which Target lists as a coconut allergen on the Island Mango tub, and colors from plant sources such as turmeric and annatto.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Per serving (Island Mango label)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Amount</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Scoop size</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">26.3 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Carbohydrate / sugar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 g / 1 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0.5 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sodium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10 mg</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweetener</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It is not lactose-free</h3>

          <p>
            Oath's own answers in Target's Q&A state that the clear whey naturally contains lactose and is not lactose-free or casein-free. Whey isolate is usually far lower in lactose than concentrate, and many people with mild intolerance handle it fine, but if you are sensitive, start with a single-serve packet rather than a full tub. Our explainer on <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> covers why isolate is usually easier to digest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Oath Protein Lineup, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            These are the Oath protein products we could confirm on the brand's own site and on major retailer listings at the time of writing, flagship first, followed by two clearly labelled alternatives from other brands. Nutrition figures are per the label or the brand's published claims at the time of writing and vary by flavor, so check the panel on the exact flavor you buy.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="which-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Oath Protein to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you came to Oath for clear whey, buy Clear Whey Isolate in the 18 or 20 serving size, in whichever flavor appeals. It is the product the brand is built around, the most widely available, and the one with the most buyer feedback. The 18 serving tub on Amazon is the easiest to buy and frequently discounted, and the brand's own pouches are often cheaper still during site-wide sales.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want a traditional milkshake-style protein from the same brand, Oath Whey Protein is the better fit. Oath's page lists 25 g of protein per serving, 12.2 g of essential amino acids and 2.7 g of leucine, in flavors such as Chocolate Dream, Chocolate Brownie and Vanilla Bean Ice Cream, with 25 servings per bag. Several flavors were sold out on Oath's site when we checked, and the supplement panel was only published as an image, so we could not confirm calories or the sweetener system. Check the panel when you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            The single-serve packets and the 12-count variety pack are the right way to test flavors before committing. Clear whey is polarizing: some buyers love its juice-like taste, others find it too sweet or too acidic. Spending a few dollars to try three or four flavors is cheaper than abandoning a full tub. As a daily habit, though, packets cost noticeably more per gram than tubs.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Sparkling Energy plus Protein cans are a different product altogether. With 10 g of protein and 100 mg of caffeine per can, they are a protein-boosted energy drink rather than a protein supplement, and they are a poor way to hit a protein target. Treat them as an alternative to an energy drink, not to a shake. Oath's electrolytes, pre-workout and creatine sit outside the scope of this review.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are torn between clear whey and regular whey in general, our <a href="/best-clear-protein-powder/">best clear protein powder</a> guide ranks the clear options against each other, and the regular-whey pillar linked above covers the milkshake side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cost per 25 g of Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cost per 25 g of protein is the fairest way to compare powders with different scoop sizes. Take the price, divide by servings, then multiply by 25 divided by the protein per serving. For Oath Clear Whey, which has 20 g per scoop, that adds 25 percent to the per-scoop price, so a 2.20 dollar scoop becomes about 2.80 dollars per 25 g of protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            Prices at the time of writing ranged widely. Target and Academy listed 18 serving tubs at about 35 to 40 dollars, which works out to roughly 2.40 to 2.80 dollars per 25 g. Deal sites tracked the Amazon 18 serving tub at around 28 to 30 dollars with Subscribe and Save, or roughly 2 dollars per 25 g. Oath's own site showed its 20 serving pouches discounted to around 22 dollars from a 45 dollar regular price, which would be about 1.40 dollars per 25 g at the sale price.
          </p>

