        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Testing and Quality Claims
          </h2>

          <p>
            Oath's quality claims are reasonable but thin on documentation. In Target's Q&A, the brand says its clear whey is made in the United States in cGMP-certified facilities and undergoes third-party testing. cGMP refers to the FDA's current good manufacturing practice rules, which every supplement maker is expected to follow, so it is a baseline rather than a distinction. The brand's product pages we checked did not name a specific certification program.
          </p>

          <p>
            We could not find Oath products listed with NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Choice or Informed Sport at the time of writing. Those programs test for banned substances and publish searchable databases, which is what drug-tested athletes need. If you compete in a tested sport, buy a clear whey that carries one of those marks and check your lot in the certifier's database.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everyone else, the risk profile of a whey isolate is generally low. Dairy proteins tend to test lower for heavy metals than plant proteins, and isolate is a highly refined ingredient. Oath's labels are also simple and specific, listing the protein source, sweetener and acids clearly, which is more than some flavor-led brands manage. What is missing is independent proof you can look up yourself.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHAT TO ASK FOR</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Third-party testing claims are only as good as the results you can see.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If testing matters to you, ask the brand which lab tests its batches, what it tests for, and whether results are published per lot. A named certification with a public database is the standard to compare against.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Our guide on whether <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder is safe</a> covers how to read testing claims and what the contaminant data across the category actually shows.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Who Should Skip Oath
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip Oath if you are a drug-tested athlete. Without a certification we could confirm, there is no public database to check a lot against, and that is the one thing tested athletes cannot compromise on. Whichever clear whey you consider instead, search the NSF and Informed Sport databases for the exact product and flavor before you buy, because certification status varies by product and market.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you avoid sucralose or artificial flavors. The Island Mango panel lists sucralose, and the Frosty Lemonade listing mentions natural and artificial flavors. Some clear wheys use stevia or monk fruit, but most of the category relies on sucralose to cover the acidity, so you may have to accept a smaller selection if this matters to you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you are lactose intolerant enough that even isolate bothers you, or if you have a coconut allergy, since some flavors contain coconut milk powder. Skip it if you want the most protein per dollar: at full retail, a standard whey blend delivers more protein per scoop for less. And skip clear whey generally if you want a creamy, filling shake, because it is designed to taste like a drink, not a dessert.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, skip it if you have a known kidney condition, are pregnant or take medication without checking first. Protein powder is a food supplement, but a large change in protein intake is worth discussing with a doctor or registered dietitian. Nothing on this page is medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

