        <div id="using" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Getting the Most From Oath Clear Whey
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clear whey foams when shaken, and buyers report the same with Oath. The common fixes are to add the powder to cold water rather than the other way round, shake gently or stir, and let it sit for a minute so the foam settles. Some people mix it in a large glass with a spoon instead of a shaker bottle. Using very cold water or ice tends to improve both the texture and the taste.
          </p>

          <p>
            Drink it fairly soon after mixing. Oath's own Q&A notes that the product can separate if it sits, and recommends shaking between sips. That is normal for clear whey, which is a thin solution rather than a stable emulsion, and it is one reason the category suits a quick post-workout drink better than a slow sip at a desk.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because each scoop holds 20 g, consider a scoop and a half after training if you want a dose closer to 30 g, or pair it with a protein-rich meal. Clear whey also works well in places where a creamy shake would not, such as mixed into sparkling water, frozen into ice pops or blended with ice into a slush. Avoid mixing it with milk: the acidity can curdle it, and that defeats the point of buying a clear protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are using protein for weight management, clear whey's low calorie count and light texture can make it easier to fit in. If you are using it to gain weight or muscle on a budget, a regular whey or a gainer is the more efficient tool. Our <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> will tell you how many scoops your target actually needs.
          </p>
        </div>

