          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a full tub before trying clear whey</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Clear whey is polarizing. Some people love the juice-like taste, others find it too sweet or too acidic. Buy single-serve packets or the variety pack first, decide which flavor you actually enjoy, and only then commit to a tub, so you do not end up with a nearly full container in the cupboard.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying full retail price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Oath runs frequent sales on its own site and on Amazon, and the gap is large. At full retail the clear whey costs roughly 2.80 dollars per 25 g of protein, while sale prices have dropped it well under 2 dollars. Wait for a promotion or use Subscribe and Save if you buy it regularly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming third-party testing means certification</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Oath says its products undergo third-party testing, but we could not find a named certification with a public database, such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice. Drug-tested athletes need a certified lot they can look up, so treat a general testing claim as a starting point, not proof.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing clear whey with milk</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Clear whey is acidified so it dissolves into a thin drink. Mixing it with milk can curdle it and wastes what you paid extra for. Use cold water, sparkling water or ice, stir rather than shaking hard, and let the foam settle for a minute before drinking.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting one 20 g scoop as a full dose</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Each clear whey scoop holds 20 g of protein, a little short of the 25 to 30 g many lifters aim for in one sitting. Use a scoop and a half after training, or pair the shake with a protein-rich meal, so you actually reach the dose you are targeting.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
